// CInt keeps C's 32-bit sign intact: strcmp's NEGATIVE must stay negative.
// (Declared -> Int, the old i64 mapping read undefined upper bits.)
//
// This once printed the raw value and expected `-1`. That was wrong, and arm64 proved it:
// C specifies only that strcmp returns a value less than, equal to, or greater than zero —
// **the magnitude is unspecified**. glibc on x86-64 answered -1 here; on aarch64 it answered
// -32, and the same on `widths.bx` gave -64. Neither is a bug; the fixture was asserting an
// implementation detail of one libc on one architecture.
//
// The sign is what this fixture is actually about — a negative i32 read as 64 bits would be
// enormous, which is the defect CInt exists to prevent — so the sign is what it now checks.
// Burxt's own String ordering was never exposed to this: `codegen.rs` uses strcmp's sign and
// nothing else ("the sign IS the answer").
external function strcmp(a: String, b: String) -> CInt;
print(strcmp("abc", "abd") < 0);
print(strcmp("abc", "abd") < 0);
print(strcmp("same", "same") == 0);
print(strcmp("abd", "abc") > 0);
